      "encounter_type": "Office visit",
      "clinician": "Dr. M. Castellanos",
      "specialty": "Endocrinology",
      "reason": "Diabetes management, post-hypoglycaemia follow-up",
      "note": "Fifty-nine year old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus of nine years' duration, currently on a multiple daily injection regimen: insulin glargine 38 units nightly plus insulin lispro three times daily with meals, four injections per day total, alongside metformin 1000 mg twice daily. Hemoglobin A1c today 8.6 percent, up from 8.1 percent in November. Reviewed the April emergency department visit for a glucose of 48 mg/dL. Patient confirms he no longer reliably senses hypoglycaemia and describes two further episodes since April identified only because his spouse noticed the confusion. He performs fingerstick testing but concedes he checks two to three times daily rather than the six to eight the regimen requires, citing pain in the fingertips and the demands of his work as a delivery driver, during which he cannot stop to test. Because his overnight and mid-shift excursions are going undetected, fingerstick monitoring at the frequency he is able to sustain is insufficient to guide safe titration of his mealtime insulin. Plan: prescribe a personal continuous glucose monitoring system with alerts for hypoglycaemia; refer to the diabetes education team for device training; continue current insulin doses pending sensor data; return in three months.",
      "locator": "enc/2026-05-19/endocrinology",
      "provenance": "authored"
    },
